# 🏦 Smart Loan Recovery System

**Domain:** Fintech · **Skills:** Classification · Clustering · Business strategy · MLOps

Banks carry thousands of overdue loans and can't chase all of them equally hard. This project predicts
**which overdue loans will be recovered and which will turn into losses**, groups borrowers into risk
segments, and turns every prediction into a **recovery action with a rupee value attached**, served
through a REST API and a collections dashboard.

![CI](https://github.com/Pps-369/smart-loan-recovery/actions/workflows/ci.yml/badge.svg)

---

## Results (hold-out test set, 2,400 loans)

| | Model | Days-past-due rule |
|---|---|---|
| ROC-AUC | **0.820** | 0.657 |
| PR-AUC (loss class, base rate 28%) | **0.661** | 0.425 |
| Brier score (calibration) | 0.146 | – |

The question a bank actually asks is *"does this make us money?"* So escalation policies were compared by
net recovery value (recovery saved minus escalation cost):

| Escalation policy | Net value on test portfolio |
|---|---|
| **Model-driven (threshold 0.43)** | **+₹0.83 Cr** |
| Escalate if 90+ days past due (typical rule) | −₹0.96 Cr |
| Escalate every loan | −₹2.64 Cr |
| Perfect foresight (ceiling) | +₹2.53 Cr |

The model escalates 23% of loans and captures about a third of the theoretical maximum value. The common
"90+ DPD" rule loses money because it spends effort on large numbers of loans that would have recovered anyway.

> Numbers come from `reports/training_summary.md` and depend on the cost assumptions in `config.yaml`.
> The data is synthetic (see [Data](#data)).

